// Design-stage example: signatures as pairs {T :: Φ} over a shared ambient enum.
// EnumU, EnumT, Sig, switch, interpretation, in, and signature-lifting µ are
// intentionally ahead of the current parser/checker.
// In this surface sketch, type/index `()` abbreviates Unit type `1` / value
// `unit`, respectively.

// The ambient list enumeration has two labels.
ListE : EnumU
ListE = consE 'empty (consE 'new nilE).

// Enum-selection sugar used by the closed NonEmpty spelling below.  Its labels
// elaborate to the same {T :: Φ} pair as `NonEmptyLabels`.  On this closed path
// the checker resolves {'new} in the ambient enum and decides
//
//   Subset ['new] ['empty,'new]
//
// by computation.  It canonicalizes ListE first as {'empty -> 0, 'new -> 1}.
// The restricted signature therefore inherits ambient tag 1 for 'new; it does
// not renumber the singleton label to position 0.
ListNonEmpty : EnumU
ListNonEmpty = consE 'new nilE.

// There is one complete branch function for the ambient enumeration.  It is an
// ordinary expression EnumT ListE -> IDesc (), not a list of Static symbols.
ListBranches
  | A :: (A : *) ~> (EnumT ListE) ~> (IDesc ()) =>
    switch ListE
      { 'empty -> '1
      , 'new   -> ('Σ A (λ value. 'var ()))
      }.

// A signature is an ordinary pair: the complete ambient branch function
// together with the enabled-label list.  Formation is pairing, nothing else:
//
//   Sig I E   :=  (EnumT E -> IDesc I) × List (Label E)
//   {T :: Φ}  :=  (T, Φ)          {T} := {T :: []}
//
// `branches` and `labels` are the pair projections, so they compute
// judgmentally even when Φ is neutral.  The append reading is derived
// notation on the same pair:  c \append {T :: Φ} := {T :: c :: Φ}.
//
// `Label E := EnumT E`: in the core a constructor is its position in the
// ambient enumeration, so a foreign-family label is unrepresentable.  A
// closed literal such as 'new belongs to elaboration — it resolves through
// At/NoDupEnum to its position.
//
// A closed label list evaluates, so membership, subset, disjointness, and
// coverage are decided by computation; the checker's kappa table is only a
// name-resolution cache.  An open list remains a genuine neutral term and is
// manipulated using ordinary Member/Subset proofs.  `progress/2.pom` defines
// the full decision pipeline (constructors, then lists, then signatures)
// with union, subset, and decidable-disjointness examples.

// Source label lists spell names; they elaborate to positions ([0, 1+0]
// and [1+0] respectively).
ListLabels : List (Label ListE)
ListLabels = ['empty, 'new].

NonEmptyLabels : List (Label ListE)
NonEmptyLabels = ['new].

// The full signature enables every ambient label.
List
  | A :: (A : *) ~> (Sig () ListE) =>
    {(ListBranches A) :: ListLabels}.

// The restricted signature reuses the SAME complete branch function; only the
// label list changes.  The 'empty arm in switch ListE is harmless: NonEmpty
// enables only 'new, so coverage and constructor introduction consult the
// enabled list, not every arm handled by the ambient switch.
NonEmpty
  | A :: (A : *) ~> (Sig () ListE) =>
    {(ListBranches A) :: NonEmptyLabels}.

// The earlier enum-selection spelling remains convenient surface sugar:
//
//   'σ ListE        (ListBranches A)  ≡ List A
//   'σ ListNonEmpty (ListBranches A)  ≡ NonEmpty A
//
// Elaboration resolves the labels of the selection enum in the ambient enum of
// the branch function and builds the same {T :: Φ} pair.  Both spellings
// therefore construct the same signature; neither creates a parallel
// representation.

// µ uniformly lifts a signature to a head-refined type.  Recursive 'var
// fields use the ordinary ambient ListE carrier, and subtyping is
// coercion-free: a surface µ(List A) used recursively is accepted at the
// carrier by subsumption, with the term unchanged.
//
//   ListType A     = µ (List A)
//   NonEmptyType A = µ (NonEmpty A)
//
// Constructor elaboration is directed by the expected type (conceptually):
//
//   empty : µ (List A)
//   empty = in 'empty unit
//
//   new : A -> µ (List A) -> µ (NonEmpty A)
//   new value tail = in 'new (value, tail)
//
// The elaborated term stores the resolved ambient position in the term
// itself: `in 'new payload` is core `in (1+0, payload)`.  Enabled
// membership is the paper's C ∈ Φ side condition, checked against the
// evaluated enabled list and stored nowhere — the refinement is a phantom
// type, so the source/runtime representation stays compact and no side
// table exists.
//
// The tail is the full list carrier, not the restricted carrier.  Therefore:
//
//   oneThenEmpty : µ (NonEmpty Nat)
//   oneThenEmpty = in 'new (1, in 'empty unit)

// Supplying a new head makes an arbitrary list non-empty.  The enabled 'new
// branch has payload Σ(value : A). 'var (), and ambient-aware µ turns that
// recursive field into the ambient carrier.  Subsumption accepts the supplied
// surface µ(List A) tail there without changing it.
prependNE
  | A value tail ::
      (A : *) ~> ~ A ~> ~ (µ (List A)) ~> (µ (NonEmpty A)) =>
    (in 'new (value, tail)).

// In particular, prepending to empty constructs a singleton.
singletonNE
  | A value ::
      (A : *) ~> ~ A ~> (µ (NonEmpty A)) =>
    (prependNE A value (in 'empty unit)).

// Observation goes in the other direction: head is total only when its input
// is already known to expose 'new.  There is exactly one enabled outer case,
// and coverage Subset ['new] ['new] is decided by computation.
headNE
  | A xs ::
      (A : *) ~> ~ (µ (NonEmpty A)) ~> A =>
    [xs of A
      |(in 'new (value, tail)) => value
    ].

// The payload projection itself returns the ambient carrier:
//
//   tailCarrierNE : µ (NonEmpty A) -> Carrier (ListS A) ()
//
// A surface `tailNE : µ(NonEmpty A) -> µ(List A)` additionally applies
// `coverFull`, whose full-coverage Subset proof re-establishes the full
// signature on every ambient tag.  Neither result is µ(NonEmpty A): the
// one-element list has empty as its tail.

// There is no total parametric conversion
//
//   µ (List A) -> µ (NonEmpty A)
//
// without also supplying a head value or successfully matching the input's
// outer tag as 'new.  An operation that merely tests an arbitrary list must
// return an optional result instead.

// Expected lifted-signature relations:
//
//   µ (NonEmpty A)  ⊑  µ (List A)
//   µ (List A)      ⋢  µ (NonEmpty A)
//
// The shared branch functions are compared as functions, normalizing modulo
// alpha, beta, and eta.  For example, this eta-expanded branch expression
// denotes the same restricted signature:
//
//   {(λ (e : EnumT ListE). ListBranches A e) :: NonEmptyLabels}

// If µ instead took the ordinary fixed point of only ListNonEmpty, every tail
// would also have to start with 'new and finite lists could never end in
// empty.  The ambient-aware interpretation above is therefore part of µ's
// contract.
